{
  "image_name": "000b79cee762386511b1b659aa07a996.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a workflow for studying the autoimmune T‑cell receptor (TCR) repertoire, moving from broad repertoire profiling toward targeted identification of disease‑relevant receptors and antigens. On the left, large‑scale methods include low‑resolution approaches such as Vβ‑specific flow cytometry, spectratyping, and biased VDJ usage analysis, followed by bulk TCR sequencing of DNA or RNA to measure diversity, clonality, and CDR3 features. More focused methods then narrow the search: single‑cell RNA sequencing links individual TCRs to phenotypic markers and cell states, while antigen screening tests receptor binding or functional reactivity using peptide display or cell‑based assays.\n\nThe center-right shows this as a funnel: a broad, polyclonal autoimmune repertoire is progressively reduced to “suspected TCRβ” candidates and then to antigen-associated information. Factors that complicate this narrowing include HLA genetics, microbial cross-reactivity, antigen presentation, tissue specificity, disease stage, and phenotypic targeting. The lower part highlights the key outputs needed to connect TCRs to disease mechanisms: αβ TCR pairing, peptide–HLA context, and antigen source. Overall, the figure emphasizes how combining repertoire sequencing, single‑cell phenotyping, and antigen discovery can translate autoimmune TCR studies into biomarkers, mechanistic insight, and potential TCR‑directed or antigen‑based therapies.",
